Date: Sun, 27 Nov 1994 16:23:57 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-11-424] PREDICTION: a crucial tool in the Extropian kit

me:
>C (including the C++ variant) will still be a major computer programming 
>langauge in 2010.  [...]

Craig Presson:
> That one is almost certainly false, if you mean that C/C++ will keep its
> current "market share".

No, I just said it will still have a major market share, I did not say
it would be as great as today.  I don't expect it to be down more than
50% in 2010.

C serves a very different role than 4GLs, SQL, perl, et. al.  It is
a language of hardware/user-efficient implementation of systems and 
widely used applications programs, while the latter are for user-inefficient,
programmer-efficient custom programs.  C has been rightfully (but with 
the wrong attitude) called "glorified assembly language".  The reason 
it is so successful is because it can be used to closely mimic and
specifically manipulate the underlying architecture in an optimal way, 
while still being portable between architectures.  Until another language 
comes along that can do that substantially better, C will remain king 
of this very large, important software market.

Note that programming close to the hardware translates in rapid
response time for the user, and use of cheaper a wider variety
of hardware, so that improvement in hardware efficiency does not
mean programming close to the hardware becomes substantially less
important.  That's why, for example, the vast majority of $$$$
in software systems and applications software developed by
Microsoft, Novell, et. al. are written in C, and this will remain 
the case for perhaps decades to come (2010 is only 15 years and
a month away).

Religious war about languages aside, even if we posit that C is
obsolete, it is clear that programming languages stay around long 
after they have been declared obsolete and new superior languages have 
been developed.  COBOL, FORTRAN, and LISP are still widely used,
decades after better languages came along and these were declared 
dinosaurs.  I expect all three, especially LISP, will still be in 
use in 2010, although to a much smaller extent than they are today, as 
there is a very slow drift from FORTRAN to C or Mathematica (depending 
again on the programmer vs. hardware/user efficiency tradeoff).
It is only wishful thinking to expect this objective fact of
history to magically change, just because we desire it to be so.

