From: levy@cs.utk.edu
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 1994 22:24:48 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-11-434] FEMME: Paglia

To Nancie Clark's excellent observations about Camille Paglia, I would
add the following remarks:  After reading _Sex, Art, and American
Culture_ (reviewed in the July/August 1993 _Exponent_), I ended up liking
Paglia a lot for her brazen attacks on left-liberal "feminists", for
her profound knowledge of the Western intellectual tradition, and for
her recognition of the liberating effect that capitalism has had
on women since the Industrial Revolution.  But I got a nasty feeling about 
the other side of Paglia, the side that idolizes pop stars and seeks to
shock and provoke, with the apparent end of creating a cult of personality.
This element of Paglia seems to have come to the fore in her recent
_Vamps and Tramps_ book, whose soft-cover edition features a photo
of Paglia in full leather garb, striking a pose like a surfer or
dominatrix or something equally trite.  Perhaps she or her publisher
meant this to excite me, or fill me with a deeper understanding of the power 
of female sexuality; instead, it left me somewhat embarrassed, like
listening to some middle-aged college professor trying to immitate the
lingo of his young students, and failing.

By her own enthusiastic admission, Paglia worships mass-media figures
like Madonna, endorses the testosterone-driven antics of college
jocks, and despises female vocalists such as Suzanne Vega, who sing 
about a different side of women's experience.  I suspect that these 
tastes come as much from some desperate desire to seem hip and 
anti-academic, as they come from any well-thought out or deeply felt 
position.  You can gain far more insight into women's psychology from 
half the songs on Suzanne Vega's first album than you will ever get 
from the entire corpus of Madonna's top-forty tripe.

